# Read in tsv files
![](/images/footprint.png){style="width:150px; background:white; border-radius:5px" fig-align="center"}

For our next task we will __read__ a tab separated file. 

Download [Global_eco_footprint.tsv](https://neof-workshops.github.io/R_j4c0xh/downloads/Global_eco_footprint.tsv) into your "files_and_subsetting" folder/directory. 
This file contains the 2023 global ecological footprint information. 
The ecological footprint measures the ecological assets that a given population requires to produce the natural resources it consumes.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Global_eco_footprint.tsv"}
More info can be found in the following link: <https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/jainaru/global-ecological-footprint-2023>.
:::

## sep = "\\t"

Use `read.csv()` to __read__ in the file. We'll set the option `row.names = 1` again but also include the option `sep = "\t"`. This option specifies the columns are separated (`sep`) by tabs (`"\t"`).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
global_eco_footprint_df <- read.csv("Global_eco_footprint.tsv", 
                        row.names = 1, sep = "\t")
```
:::


## check.names  = FALSE

Look at the resulting <u id='data_frame_tooltip'>__data frame__</u> and you will notice that the column names have been changed by R. This is annoying but thankfully there is an easy fix. __Read__ in the data again with the inclusion of the parameter `check.names = FALSE`. This will stop the function `read.csv()` from 'checking' and 'fixing' the column names. I always use this option.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
global_eco_footprint_df <- read.csv("Global_eco_footprint.tsv", 
                                 row.names = 1, sep = "\t", 
                                 check.names = FALSE)
```
:::



